{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nMutation testing should protect changed behavioral cores, but the historical whole-file counts are stale. A pinned mutmut 3.7.0 probe found 814 mutants in `signing.py` versus 491 in the old record, including both equivalent survivors and real contract gaps. Enforcing the old ceilings would block unrelated work; accepting all current survivors would normalize defects.\n\n## Plan\n\nUse `.github/mutation-shards.toml` as the sole source for shard mappings, reviewed equivalent fingerprints, and an explicit per-shard enforcement mode:\n\n- `advisory`: run head mutation evidence, publish every outcome, and do not fail Gerrit for mutation-quality findings. Configuration, selection, and artifact-contract failures still fail because no evidence was produced.\n- `ratchet`: compare fresh base/head archives. For AST-identical functions, preserve existing non-killed outcomes but block a killed mutant becoming non-killed, a killed mutant disappearing, any new survivor/no-tests result, and every timeout or incomplete status. Added or edited functions must kill every mutant unless a survivor matches a reviewed equivalent fingerprint.\n- `strict`: apply the changed-function rule to the entire shard: every mutant is killed or individually fingerprinted as equivalent; no-tests, timeouts, incomplete statuses, and zero mutants block.\n\nStart all legacy shards as advisory. Promote a shard to ratchet only after several successful runs establish stable IDs, runtime, and mappings. Promote it to strict after its legacy outcomes are remediated or individually reviewed. Mode changes are small, reviewable manifest changes; there is no fuzzy severity classification.\n\nA selector inspects `HEAD^..HEAD`, including both sides of renames. Mapped source/test/support changes select their shards; mutation infrastructure changes select all. Unmapped changed Python tests are advisory. Unresolved refs/diffs fail; an empty selection is an explicit successful skip.\n\nThe reusable workflow creates one matrix job per selected shard, each with Python 3.12, `mutmut==3.7.0`, `--max-children 1`, the configured mutmut timeout, and a 30-minute job limit. Dependency downloads may be cached; mutation verdict state may not. Selected clean tests run before mutation. Gerrit uses the exact patchset and includes the reusable in `vote.needs`; the push/PR lane mirrors it. A weekly/manual sweep selects every shard through the same manifest and matrix.\n\nMutation-result reruns are diagnostic only and cannot turn red green. Only independently identified setup failures may retry. Raw logs, survivor diffs/fingerprints, and a structured summary publish for every driver outcome. The existing two-vote rollback runbook is the emergency recovery path.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Selection tests cover source/test/support/global inputs, renames/deletions, unmatched tests, empty selection, and unresolved refs.\n- [ ] Advisory tests prove mutation-quality findings are reported but do not fail; missing evidence still fails.\n- [ ] Ratchet tests prove unchanged legacy survivors/no-tests are grandfathered while killed regressions, missing killed mutants, new non-killed outcomes, timeouts, and incomplete statuses block.\n- [ ] Ratchet tests prove added/edited functions require 100% killed or a reviewed equivalent fingerprint.\n- [ ] Strict tests prove the whole shard meets the same clean rule.\n- [ ] Execution tests prove fresh archives, no verdict-cache restore, pinned runtime/concurrency/timeouts, clean-test preflight, and zero-mutant detection.\n- [ ] Workflow tests prove one bounded job per selected shard, exact Gerrit patchset checkout, `vote.needs`, push/PR parity, weekly/manual all-shard selection, cancellation, and always-published artifacts.\n- [ ] A real compact-policy smoke run passes from fresh base/head archives.\n- [ ] Documentation explains the three modes, promotion evidence, false-positive policy, cadence, and manifest authority without duplicating shard data.\n\n## Verification\n\n    pytest -q tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py\n    python scripts/mutation_gate.py smoke --shard compact-policy --base HEAD^ --head HEAD\n    python scripts/check_verify_gate_parity.py\n    make lint\n    make typecheck\n\n## Primary evidence\n\nCurrent primary-source examples: Instructor uses a pinned bounded shard with explicit exceptions; DataDog maps changed tests to source and compares base/head; PostHog gates a targeted PR shard; Trailmark cancels superseded runs and always reports; GeoParquet schedules broad mutation, fails on missing evidence, retries setup only, and publishes artifacts. The real pinned-tool probe and correction are recorded in the ticket comments."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786423792726487001,"uuid":"ac4bd1ef-afce-4fa1-8da4-eb9f79faee49"}